ts on stdin.\n- Each run logs its start time and `claude exited <rc> after <N>s`, which goes\n  to `nightly-review.out`. A run that finishes in a few seconds is visibly\n  suspicious.\n- Exit 0 with an empty output file is treated as a failure and reported. A green\n  exit with nothing done is no longer a success.\n\n`Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist`\n- Added `USER` = `vk` under `EnvironmentVariables`. I took `vk` from the paths\n  already hard-coded in the plist (`/Users/vk/...`). If your short username is\n  different, change it.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\nApplied, and confirmed by this job's logs:\n- zsh `status` is read-only, and assigning to it kills the script before the\n  failure notice. This is exactly what happened here.\n- The notifier sat downstream of the failure. This is the key lesson.\n- The \"Not logged in\" trap: the CLI needs `USER` in the plist, and that was\n  missing here.\n- `launchctl print` shows the runs count and last exit code. Here they proved\n  the job fired on schedule, which contradicted the belief that launchd was not\n  firing.\n\nApplied as defensive hardening, not evidenced by these logs:\n- `< /dev/null` on `claude`. The prompt is passed as an argument, so this is a\n  precaution against stdin waits. The logs show no such hang.\n- Logging each run's duration and checking that the output is non-empty.\n\nDid not apply:\n- \"Input must be provided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\". That\n  error does not appear in these logs. The prompt is passed as a quoted argument,\n  and the script is unchanged in that respect, so the stdin-pipe fix was not\n  needed.\n\nThe colleague's claim that a failing exit code is trustworthy is what this case\nsupports: here `last exit code = 1` matched the real failure. Their correction\nstill holds: a 0 exit with no work is possible, so the output check was added.\n\n## Not verified here\n\n- I could not run the job under real launchd. This folder is a snapshot, and a\n  real run would send a Telegram message, so I stayed in a scratch home with a\n  fake `claude` and a fake notifier. The \"USER unset causes Not logged in\"\n  connection is inferred from the logs and the colleague's note, not reproduced\n  against the real CLI.\n- `notify.sh` reads its token from the keychain with `security`. The README says\n  it works by hand. Whether it works from launchd is untested until you run the\n  success test below.\n- `claude` is at `/opt/homebrew/bin/claude`, which is on the plist's PATH.\n\n## What you need to do to deploy\n\nI did not deploy anything. On your machine:\n\n```sh\ncp bin/nightly-review.zsh ~/bin/nightly-review.zsh\ncp Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/\nlaunchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.vk.nightly-review\nlaunchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\nlaunchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.vk.nightly-review\n```\n\nThen check:\n1. `launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.vk.nightly-review` shows `last exit code = 0`.\n2. `nightly-review.out` shows a duration that is plausible for a real review, not a\n   few seconds.\n3. The Telegram success message arrives, and `nightly-review-<date>.md` is fresh.\n\nTo confirm failure is also visible, temporarily break the script (for example,\npoint `REPO` at a missing directory, or make `claude` fail), kickstart again,\nand confirm the failure message arrives. Then restore the script.\n\n## Takeaway\n\nWhen automation is silent, first prove whether it ran, using `launchctl print`\nand its logs. Then check whether the failure reporter sits downstream of the\nfailure. Here it did, and a single zsh reserved word silenced it.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01H7yhXzb2nbz4ew7cbEkWsg","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
